¶ XVIII.

Footnote: JUST INTOXATIOX IN SIXGING.

(See p. 326i.)

¶ Since the publication of the first edition of this book, I have had an opportunity ¶ seeing the Enharmonic Organ, constructed by General Perronet Thompson,;}; ¶ allows of performance in 21 major and minor scales with different tonics ¶ connected. This instrument is much more complicated than my ¶ It contains 40 pipes to the octave, and has three distinct manuals, ¶ on the whole, 65 digitals to the octave, as the same note has to be sometimes

¶ struck on two or all of the manuals. This instrument allows of the performance ¶ much more extensive modulations than my harmonium, without requiring any ¶ interchanges. It is even possible to execute tolerably quick passages ¶ ornamentations upon it, notwithstanding its apparently involved fingering.

¶ [The E minor has the leading note, but ¶ the minor Seventh. The other minor ¶ have both. — Translator.]

¶ [As Prof. Heknholtz has retained this ¶ in his 4th Gemian edition it is ¶ in the translation. But the scheme ¶ explained has never been tried. The plan for ¶ notes is impracticable because of the de¶ state of the minor keys, and imperfect ¶ power. It could only be used ¶ an experimental instrument, and for that ¶ double keyboard as explained on p. 316?) ¶ The valve arrangement for 30 notes ¶ be complicated, and even if it could be ¶ would still have a very imperfect modu¶ power. The .53 division of the octave ¶ by ilr. Bosauquet, and subse¶ by Mr. Paul White, with fingerboards ¶ have been actually used, as explained ¶ App. XIX., and also App. XX. sect. F. ¶ 8 and 9, are so much superior in mani¶ musical effect, and power of modula¶ that it is unnecessai-y -to seek further. ¶ App. XX. sect. F. the other principal ¶ that have been actually tried, are

