J [' On the principles and Practice of Just Intonation, with a view to the Abolition of Temperament, and embodying the results of the Tonic Sol-fa Associations, as illustrated on the Enharmonic Organ . . . presenting the power of performing correctly in 21 keys (with the minors to the extent of involving not more than five flats), and a correction for changes of temperature. . . . Calculated for taking the place of the choir organ in a cathedral, and learned by the blind in six lessons. ¶ With an Appendix tracing the identity of sign with the Enharmonic of the Ancients.' By T. Perronet Thompson, F.R.S. Ninth edition, 1866. The exact compass of this organ will be explained in App. XX. sect. F. No. 6. General Thompson was born at Hull, in 1783. and died at Blackheath, 6 September, 1869. He had been four years in the navy before joining the army, and was prominent during the Corn Law Abolition agitation. He was many years editor of the Westminster Review, and was first returned to Parliament for Hull in lSi5.— Translator.']
